{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Seattle Mariners opened the year with a turbulent April, stumbling through early losses as their bullpen leaked runs and Carlos Vargas proved to be a recurring thorn. A rough series against the Giants on April 4th, followed by shaky outings from Gregory Santos, cast early doubt on the club's playoff ambitions. Yet the Mariners refused to fold, with Jorge Polanco stepping up in clutch moments and Julio Rodríguez flashing his star potential with a big April double that kept Seattle competitive through the rough patches.\n\nThe summer months brought a steady climb. J.P. Crawford emerged as an unlikely offensive force, launching home runs at key moments, while Randy Arozarena proved his worth as a big-game performer. Cal Raleigh's monster season behind the plate — capped by a three-run shot in August that stands as one of the most electrifying swings of the year — anchored the lineup. Eugenio Suárez added thunder of his own, slugging his way to 41 home runs and providing the middle-of-the-order presence the team needed to stay in the AL West race.\n\nSeattle's stretch run in September was defined by resilience. Josh Naylor's clutch double on September 23rd exemplified a team that had learned to manufacture runs when it mattered most. The Mariners closed out the regular season at 90-72, a testament to their balance of a 3.87 ERA pitching staff and a lineup that slugged 238 home runs. They were a team built for October, and when the Division Series arrived, they dispatched their opponents in dramatic fashion, with Leo Rivas delivering a key single in the deciding game against Detroit.\n\nThe ALCS, however, proved to be a bridge too far. Eduard Bazardo's inability to hold late leads — echoing his rough regular-season cameos — became the defining storyline of Seattle's postseason demise. A George Springer three-run homer on October 20th extinguished what remained of Mariners' hopes, ending the season in the Championship Series. Still, for a franchise that has long chased October glory, a 90-win season and an ALCS appearance represents genuine progress, and the core of Raleigh, Rodríguez, Arozarena, and Polanco gives Seattle every reason to believe the best is still to come.",
  "chapters": [
